dot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373307 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373308 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373309 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373310 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373311 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1041 "selection" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373311 13194139535899 true] [17592186373306 1110 #uuid "643013f6-8bae-5e08-b896-f07d62d75f51" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373307 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373307 1111 #uuid "03b1599d-0467-5134-a65d-97057feeb760" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373308 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373308 1111 #uuid "009339ef-77bf-52bb-98e9-2022534d35d7" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373309 1034 "I only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373309 1111 #uuid "3e3bafb2-9057-5633-a603-cfe84a3e2444" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373310 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373310 1111 #uuid "7ff346cc-73f6-5508-b35f-f8b58699d2a7" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373311 1034 "II only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373311 1111 #uuid "f359d0de-ab92-5838-9650-b8e7d7e0b177" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373312 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373313 13194139535899 true] [17592186373312 1089 "q-107587" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373312 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535899 true] [17592186373312 1100 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373312 1113 #uuid "8c6a9bae-2247-570a-8c31-28b8f33e2aae" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373312 1145 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535899 true] [