true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373319 1113 #uuid "94559848-e5fe-50c8-8ca9-fb3c9c5e2d05" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373319 1145 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+({x}_{2}-{x}_{3}){b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(3{c}_{2})+{x}_{2}{c}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}_{1}){c}_{2}+{x}_{2}{c}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{c}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2}$ and ${b}_{3}.$ This means that ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373321 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373322 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373323 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373324 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373325 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1041 "selection" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373324 13194139535899 true] [17592186373320 1110 #uuid "3345236a-0f4a-5226-8d8b-40b79f3f3de7" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373321 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373321 1111 #uuid "33d5f059-e8e9-5169-8fd0-147df27b5c0d" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373322 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373322 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373322 1111 #uuid "559c252f-a4f6-5ebc-b273-bc77ea2367df" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373323 1034 "I only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373323 1111 #uuid "966ca0b1-71b4-5fb8-ba94-7c22bfab4602" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373324 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373324 1111 #uuid "180d7673-cf2b-5adc-bc2f-3fad36654bbf" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373325 1034 "II only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373325 1111 #uuid "e7934f01-9764-57cd-8442-4ad7d5d48f18" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373326 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373327 13194139535899 true] [17592186373326 1089 "q-94870" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373326 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535899 true] [17592186373326 1100 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373326 1113 #uuid "915e0acc-53f3-5fe1-a696-c7959bf054a1" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373326 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373328 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373329 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373330 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373331 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373332 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1041 "selection" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373328 13194139535899 true] [17592186373327 1110 #uuid "398d819c-977f-55b2-b3a1-00848cfdd6b6" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373328 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373328 1111 #uuid "1001aead-606a-5696-af1d-234b601dc4b3" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373329 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373329 1111 #uuid "73d0d43f-b1b5-51f6-bed6-5c4c96a2547b" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373330 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535899 true] [17592186373330 1111 #uuid "1f888614-42ac-540f-b459-432a82c1cd0d" 13194139535899 true] [17592186373331 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535899 tru